                                               To sleep, perchance to dream.
                              Ay there's the rub, for in that sleep of death
                              what dreams may come when we have shuffled off
                                       this mortal coil, must give us pause.


Yes, time heals all wounds; so does sleep, for a time. And is not death that
great sleep that takes away suffering, that ends pain, that brings loneli-
ness to a close? Truly it is the end and needs no healing. Dr. Spork knew
this, and he would bring and end to suffering in the lands of the dungeons
of doom, and all would sleep that sleep in the lands beyond the veil; but
Spork had even mightier plans, and he would vanquish Death itself, to remove
pain and hunger and disease and death from this world forevermore.



Dr. Spork was an extinctionist healer -- BOOOORING -- who made a few 
mistakes in the extinctionist bit in the end, which kind of ticked me off.
The main mistake was hitting the portal on water too soon, so that a bunch
of ; and my pet T were left behind. These needed to be dealt with 
retroactively on astral. That led to having many more genocides than I
really wanted. In order to have a 'broke all conducts' conduct without
endangering my planned 'extinctionist with blood on his hands' conduct, 
I had already genocided woodchucks with my first ?oGeno. So that went
out the window. I cleaned up astral with some more genocides, too.
All those xorns behind the walls were bugging me. And in the end I may
have left a single ghost on astral, too. I may have missed a message
when entering the temple the last time. The riders had rotted away by
then, and I know I left nothing alive in the main dungeon -- so I might
claim that in the _world_, Dr. Spork had taken death away from the
creatures of the world.

As a stupid ascension trick, Dr. Spork brought along dragon scales
in all the colours, to wear over his white doctor's coat.

As a detraction from the value of this ascension, Dr. Spork occasionally
copied his save file to another machine where it was loaded, followed by
#quit, yes, no, no, yes, quit to see how many monsters had been vanquished
already (I mentioned this in a previous post already -- I don't think
knowing that you have killed 97 giant ants and have another 23 to go is
particularly sensitive information, but other, purer players may disagree).

Dr. Spork lost 30 points of protection on Astral by killing the high 
priests. You murderer! No, no, he was taking away the possibility of
pain, disease, old age; that sleep of death is truly one of bliss.

Now I've ascended a healer, finally, I can get on with the three remaining
classes in my ascend-everyone-in-reverse-alphabetical-order, at long last.





